{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And HaShem spoke to Moses, saying.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the addition of 'saying' — that is, saying to Israel. Or he added 'saying' concerning the hidden, for all the words of Torah contain a revealed and a hidden; the revealed is the plain sense of the commandments, and the hidden is what is inward within it, which the mind of the multitude is not worthy of. Thus David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 62:12): ״אחת דבר אלהים שתים זו שמעתי״ — one thing has Elohim spoken, two have I heard — for the utterance is one yet contains two meanings. And so Solomon said (Proverbs 25:11): ״תפוחי זהב במשכיות כסף״ and so forth; he likened the revealed to silver and the inner to gold, which is hidden within the silver and superior to it." "<b>And here.</b> This is the meaning of 'and HaShem spoke to Moses, saying' in most places of the Torah: 'and HaShem spoke to Moses' is the plain sense of the commandment; 'saying' — that He reveal to him the hidden within it." "<b>And the Ramban.</b> Of blessed memory, he wrote in the section of Va'era: 'and HaShem spoke to Moses, saying' — in complete speech, that is, an utterance that is visible and not doubtful, because his prophecy was clear and not doubtful, mouth to mouth, and in a vision and not in riddles.'" "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: 'and HaShem spoke to Moses' is the inner, concealed power; 'saying' — through the Shekhinah. And this is what is written (Exodus 24:12): ״אשר כתבתי להורתם״ — which I have written to instruct them — missing a ו״ו, from the root of (Song of Songs 3:4) ״ואל חדר הורתי״ — and into the chamber of her who conceived me. And you already know that 'speaking' (דבור) is more inward than 'saying' (אמירה), for 'speaking' is the Written Torah and 'saying' is the Oral Torah; this is known to the masters of the Kabbalah. And this is what the masters of truth expounded (Lamentations 2:17): ״בצע אמרתו״ — this is His purpira. <b>And you should be roused</b> to reflect how they derive it from the Aramaic אמרא, and purpira means a mantle, that is, the edge of the mantle. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded (Isaiah 5:24): ״כי מאסו את תורת ה׳ צבאות״ — this is the Written Torah; ״ואת אמרת קדוש ישראל נאצו״ — this is the Oral Torah. And the explanation of the terms is: ויאמר is from אמרא, which is the edge of the mantle that a person dons on the left side, in the manner of what is written (I Samuel 28:14): ״והוא עוטה מעיל״ — and he is wrapped in a mantle — and wrapping is on the left side; and this is what is written (Psalms 104:2): ״עוטה אור כשלמה״ — wrapping Himself in light like a garment — and this is the attribute of judgment. But וידבר is from the root דבורה (the bee), and it is the Written Torah, which embraces the attribute of mercy and the attribute of judgment, for the Oral Torah emanates from it; and just as the bee contains both sweetness and a sting, so the Written Torah embraces mercy and judgment. And understand this."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {3 "Rabbeinu Bahya reads וידבר as from the same root as דבורה ('bee'), a wordplay linking the Written Torah to the bee's dual nature of sweetness and sting."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:58:33.177501808Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}